\abstract{
We study admissions with one open category and several reserve categories when a student may qualify for several at once. Under one-to-one counting an admitted student fills at most one category, so a mechanism must decide both who is admitted and which category each fills. We add a score constraint bounding the gap between the open cutoff and each reserve cutoff. Non-wastefulness must then be restated relative to it, and we introduce across-category fairness, admitting a complaint only when preference and adjusted score justify it together. Score-elevated deferred acceptance is strategy-proof and free of justified envy but need not be score-feasible: routing a multiply eligible student can itself decide whether the cutoff rule survives. We then show that score-feasibility, open-completeness and reserve-completeness may be jointly unattainable, through a Hall-type obstruction on non-singleton category sets. Adaptive score-threshold deferred acceptance idles reserve seats and stays fair across categories; preference-aware overlapping de-reservation keeps every surviving seat filled but gives up fixed capacities, strategy-proofness and across-category fairness. A prototype prices the two against each other.
}

\keywords{Matching, Affirmative Action, Reserve Categories, Overlapping Types, De-Reservation, School Choice}

