\section{\uppercase{Introduction}}
Reserve-based admissions and controlled school choice are studied extensively \cite{Abdu05a,EHYY14a,HYY13a,EcYe15a,AyTu20a,AyTu23a}. In standard formulations each student belongs to one protected category or none; in many real systems categories overlap. S\"onmez and Yenmez treat overlapping reserves within the vertical--horizontal system of Indian affirmative action \cite{SoYe22a}; related environments appear in \cite{KHIY17a,AyBo21a,DKT23a,CGH20a} and, under distributional constraints, in \cite{GKK+17a,KaKo24a}. Indian engineering admissions, the setting closest to ours, are described in \cite{BCC+19b}. That \emph{which} slot a student is counted against is itself a design variable is explicit in \cite{KoSo16a}, and the cost of getting it wrong is documented in \cite{DKPS18a}; the processing order of reserve categories is likewise consequential \cite{PSUY24a,PRS23a}, a sensitivity that reappears in PA-ODR.

Once categories overlap, a mechanism must decide not only who is admitted but how overlapping students are counted, since under one-to-one counting such a student fills at most one reserve slot: the category she is assigned is part of the allocation. We add a score constraint keeping the open and reserve cutoffs close. This is not stylized---in \emph{Ashoka Kumar Thakur vs.\ Union of India} the Supreme Court of India recommended a maximum ten-point gap between the open and OBC cutoffs, analyzed in \cite{AyTu23b}, and cutoff concerns appear more generally in \cite{AyTu23a,ABB24a,HuZh25a}. Under such a rule a reserve category may be nominally available yet unusable.

These ingredients hit the standard desiderata \cite{EHYY14a,KHIY17a,GKK+17a} asymmetrically. Non-wastefulness must be restated, an empty reserve category being possibly unusable; a complaint by an \emph{unmatched} student need not be, since replacing a reserve admit by a higher-scoring eligible one only raises that category's cutoff (Lemma~\ref{lem:fair-sf}). Once a matched student may complain the weakening has content again, and the notion we settle on---across-category fairness---is what separates our mechanisms. With overlap a high-score student eligible for several categories can protect the cutoff in only one, being counted once.

