S\"onmez and Yenmez \cite{SoYe22a} take reserve capacities as given and impose no cutoff-gap requirement, the constraint driving every negative result here. Ayg\"un and Turhan \cite{AyTu23b} study exactly the score-elevated policy and the ten-point rule, but without overlapping eligibility, so their mechanism never decides \emph{which} category a student fills; their hierarchical treatment of overlapping horizontal categories \cite{AyTu26a} admits multiple qualifications but is one-to-all with no cutoff-gap rule, so again no routing arises. What is new is thus not that a score-elevated mechanism can breach a cutoff-gap rule---that is the inconsistency of \cite{AyTu23b} and needs no overlap---but a second, independent cause: eligible for several categories yet counted once, a student has her routing decide whether the rule survives. Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf} exhibits and separates this cause, and Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} makes the same separation by hypothesis. The de-reservation response follows \cite{AyTu23a}.

