\section{\uppercase{Model}}
\label{sec:model}

\subsection{Primitives, Counting, Scores and Cutoffs}

We consider admissions to a single school. Let $I$ be a finite set of students, $T$ a finite set of reserve categories, $o$ the open category, and $V=T\cup\{o\}$. Each $c\in V$ has capacity $q^c\in\mathbb{N}$; each $i\in I$ has a raw score $\sigma(i)\in\R_+$, distinct across students, and a reserve-eligibility set $\T(i)\subseteq T$, with $i$ \emph{overlapping} when $|\T(i)|\ge2$. All reserve categories share a common bonus $k\ge0$, and $\delta\ge0$ is the allowed gap between the open and reserve cutoffs. The admissible set of $i$ is $V_i:=\{o\}\cup\T(i)$, over which she has a strict preference $\succ_i$, with $r_i(c)$ the rank of $c$ ($1$ = best): with a single school, students rank admission routes rather than schools.

We adopt \emph{one-to-one counting}: a matched student counts toward exactly one category chosen by the mechanism, not toward every one she is eligible for. This is the convention of \cite{KHIY17a,AyBo21a,GKK+17a}: a student qualifying under both a socioeconomic and a geographic criterion may enter by either route but cannot fill both quotas.

\begin{definition}[Matching and feasibility]
A matching is a tuple $\mu=(\mu_c)_{c\in V}$ with $\mu_c\subseteq I$; write $M(\mu):=\bigcup_{c\in V}\mu_c$ for the matched students. It is \emph{feasible} if (i) $|\mu_c|\le q^c$ for every $c\in V$, (ii) $\mu_c\cap\mu_{c'}=\emptyset$ for distinct $c,c'$, and (iii) $c\in V_i$ for every $i\in\mu_c$.
\end{definition}

Condition (ii) is one-to-one counting. The open category ranks by raw score, reserve categories by score plus the common bonus: for $c\in V_i$ the \emph{adjusted score} is $\sigma_c(i):=\sigma(i)$ if $c=o$ and $\sigma(i)+k$ if $c=t\in\T(i)$. Reserve categories thus share one ranking, differing only through eligibility, capacities and preferences; the case $k=0$ is covered throughout.

\begin{definition}[De-reservation and conservation of the total]
\label{def:dereserve}
An \emph{effective capacity vector} is $\hat q=(\hat q^c)_{c\in V}$ with $\hat q^t\le q^t$ for every $t\in T$ and $\sum_{c\in V}\hat q^c=\sum_{c\in V}q^c$, equivalently $\hat q^o=q^o+\sum_{t\in T}(q^t-\hat q^t)$. A mechanism \emph{de-reserves} when it operates at some $\hat q\neq q$; feasibility, cutoffs and non-wastefulness are read against the vector it operates at, which is $\hat q=q$ for Sections~\ref{sec:spse} and~\ref{sec:tada}.
\end{definition}

The only discipline we impose on capacity change is conservation of the total: seats are relabelled from reserve to open, never created or destroyed. The enrolment target is an external commitment while the split across categories is an internal instrument, so de-reservation redistributes rather than resizes, and judging a mechanism at $\hat q$ instead of $q$ costs the quota and not the seat---the entering class is as large, only the route in has moved. The inequality $\hat q^t\le q^t$ makes it one-directional, so no reserve quota is inflated at the open category's expense; Section~\ref{sec:experiments} shows the cost in quota is real nonetheless.

\begin{definition}[Cutoffs and score-feasibility]
\label{def:scorefeas}
For a matching $\mu$ let $e_o(\mu):=\min_{i\in\mu_o}\sigma(i)$, with $e_o(\mu)=-\infty$ if $\mu_o=\emptyset$, and for $t\in T$ let $e_t^+(\mu):=\min_{i\in\mu_t}\sigma_t(i)$, with $e_t^+(\mu)=+\infty$ if $\mu_t=\emptyset$. A feasible $\mu$ is \emph{score-feasible} if $e_o(\mu)-e_t^+(\mu)\le\delta$ for every $t\in T$.
\end{definition}

We read $e_o(\mu)-e_t^+(\mu)$ as $-\infty$ whenever $\mu_o$ or $\mu_t$ is empty, so the condition is vacuous there. No reserve cutoff may fall too far below the open one on the adjusted scale: the bonus is institutionally legitimate and $\delta$ bounds only the gap left after it, so the two parameters do different work rather than granting the advantage twice. In raw scores they compound, a reserve admit falling up to $\delta+k$ below the open cutoff; for $k=0$, $\delta$ alone bounds the raw gap, the ten-point rule of \cite{AyTu23b}. Score-feasibility is an ex post requirement on realized cutoffs, not implied by the ranking rule, which is why Sections~\ref{sec:tada} and~\ref{sec:paodr} enforce it directly.

Those cutoffs record the weakest admit, not the bar actually set. A category is \emph{undersubscribed} at $\mu$ when $|\mu_c|<\hat q^c$, what a school reports as an unfilled quota; having taken every applicant it received, it set no bar, so its cutoff is $0$.

\begin{definition}[Effective cutoffs]
\label{def:effcutoff}
Let $\mu$ be feasible for $\hat q$. Put $\tilde e_o(\mu):=e_o(\mu)$ if $|\mu_o|=\hat q^o\ge1$ and $\tilde e_o(\mu):=0$ otherwise; for $t\in T$ put $\tilde e_t^+(\mu):=e_t^+(\mu)$ if $|\mu_t|=\hat q^t\ge1$, $\tilde e_t^+(\mu):=0$ if $t$ is undersubscribed, and $\tilde e_t^+(\mu):=+\infty$ if $\hat q^t=0$. Then $\mu$ is \emph{effectively score-feasible} if $\tilde e_o(\mu)-\tilde e_t^+(\mu)\le\delta$ for every $t\in T$.
\end{definition}

A category holding no seats sets no bar to compare, whence the last clause: a de-reserved category leaves the constraint rather than violating it. Effective score-feasibility implies score-feasibility, as it only lowers cutoffs, strictly so by Example~\ref{ex:tada-idle}. Its bite is that $\tilde e_t^+(\mu)=0$ at an undersubscribed $t$ with $\hat q^t\ge1$ forces $\tilde e_o(\mu)\le\delta$: unless the open category sets a bar no higher than $\delta$, every surviving quota must be filled exactly and one that cannot be must be reduced. That is de-reservation, supplied at no cost in seats, so the two conventions are one design. We state results in both readings, as A-STDA satisfies only the weaker: idling a seat, its escape from Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility}, is not effectively score-feasible.

\subsection{Desiderata}

Wastefulness is usually defined by an empty category an unmatched student could occupy; here she can occupy it only when score-feasibility is preserved.

\begin{definition}[Non-wastefulness]
\label{def:nw}
A feasible matching $\mu$ is \emph{non-wasteful} if there are no unmatched $i\in I\setminus M(\mu)$ and $c\in V_i$ such that adding $i$ to $c$ yields a matching that is both feasible and score-feasible.
\end{definition}

So a reserve category may be empty yet unusable. The definition applies to feasible, not only score-feasible, matchings, since SPSE is non-wasteful without being score-feasible; and it requires the \emph{enlarged} matching to be score-feasible, not score-feasibility to be preserved, which would make an already infeasible matching vacuously non-wasteful.

Fairness admits four readings, differing in who may complain and about which category. The weakest lets only an unmatched student complain, and only about a reserve category she is eligible for.

\begin{definition}[Fairness against unmatched claimants]
\label{def:fair-unm}
A feasible matching $\mu$ is \emph{fair against unmatched claimants} if there are no $t\in T$ and students $i,j$ with (i) $t\in\T(i)\cap\T(j)$, (ii) $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$, (iii) $j\in\mu_t$, and (iv) $i\notin M(\mu)$.
\end{definition}

This is the minimal notion, isolating the guarantee that a reserve category never holds someone a stronger eligible claimant could displace. It is the only one all three mechanisms satisfy, and the only one needing no score-relative weakening.

\begin{lemma}[The unmatched claimant needs no score-relative weakening]
\label{lem:fair-sf}
Let $\mu$ be feasible, $t\in T$, $j\in\mu_t$, and $i\notin M(\mu)$ with $t\in\T(i)$ and $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$. Let $\mu'$ replace $j$ by $i$ in $\mu_t$. Then $\mu'$ is feasible, and if $\mu$ is score-feasible so is $\mu'$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Feasibility is immediate: $|\mu'_t|=|\mu_t|$, $i$ was assigned nowhere, and $t\in\T(i)\subseteq V_i$. No other category changes and $\mu_o$ is untouched, so $e_o(\mu')=e_o(\mu)$ and $e_{t'}^+(\mu')=e_{t'}^+(\mu)$ for $t'\neq t$, while $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$ gives $e_t^+(\mu')\ge e_t^+(\mu)$; hence $e_o(\mu')-e_t^+(\mu')\le\delta$.
\end{proof}

The asymmetry is genuine: adding a \emph{new} student to a reserve category can lower its cutoff, whereas \emph{replacing} one by a better student cannot, which is why only Definition~\ref{def:nw} carries a score-feasibility clause.

The remaining three let a \emph{matched} student complain. Write $\mu(i)$ for the category holding $i$, and read $c\succ_i\mu(i)$ as true when $i$ is unmatched; the first keeps the complaint inside the reserve categories.

\begin{definition}[Within-category fairness]
\label{def:fair}
A feasible matching $\mu$ is \emph{fair within categories} if there are no $t\in T$ and students $i,j$ with (i) $t\in\T(i)\cap\T(j)$, (ii) $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$, (iii) $j\in\mu_t$, and (iv) $t\succ_i\mu(i)$.
\end{definition}

Clause (iv) replaces ``$i$ is unmatched'' by ``$i$ would rather be at $t$'', so a student holding a seat may point at a category she ranks higher. Dropping the restriction to reserve categories gives the notion that separates our mechanisms.

\begin{definition}[Across-category fairness]
\label{def:across}
A feasible matching $\mu$ is \emph{fair across categories} if there are no student $i$, category $c\in V_i$ and student $j\in\mu_c$ with (i) $c\succ_i\mu(i)$ and (ii) $\sigma_c(i)>\sigma_c(j)$.
\end{definition}

A complaint is thus admitted only when justified on both counts at once, by preference and by the adjusted score of the very category envied; it is the routing of a strong overlapping student that this polices. The last notion adds the one complaint the previous two exclude, against a category holding a vacant seat.

\begin{definition}[No justified envy]
\label{def:nje}
A feasible matching $\mu$ has \emph{no justified envy} if there are no student $i$ and category $c\in V_i$ with (i) either $i\notin M(\mu)$, or $i\in\mu_{c'}$ for some $c'\in V_i$ with $c\succ_i c'$; and (ii) either $|\mu_c|<q^c$, or some $j\in\mu_c$ has $\sigma_c(j)<\sigma_c(i)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[The four notions are nested]
\label{lem:hierarchy}
No justified envy implies across-category fairness, which implies within-category fairness, which implies fairness against unmatched claimants. The first and third implications are strict.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $(i,c,j)$ block across categories. Then $c\succ_i\mu(i)$ is clause (i) of Definition~\ref{def:nje} and $j$ is the second alternative of clause (ii), so $(i,c)$ is justified envy. A within-blocking triple is an across-blocking one with $c=t\in T$, feasibility giving $t\in\T(j)$; and $i\notin M(\mu)$ implies $t\succ_i\mu(i)$. Strictness: Example~\ref{ex:tada-idle} admits justified envy yet is fair across categories, and Example~\ref{ex:paodr-envy} is fair against unmatched claimants yet not within categories; the middle implication is strict as well, Definition~\ref{def:fair} saying nothing about the open category.
\end{proof}

The three stronger notions are left unweakened, and this is a choice: Lemma~\ref{lem:fair-sf} does not extend to them. Clause (iv) of Definition~\ref{def:fair} lets a matched $i$ vacate $\mu(i)$, and for $\mu(i)=o$ that \emph{raises} the open cutoff, breaking score-feasibility elsewhere; clause (ii) of Definition~\ref{def:nje} also lets $i$ claim a \emph{vacant} category, lowering its cutoff. A weakening would thus have content for all three. Leaving them unweakened strengthens the positive results; the price is that Examples~\ref{ex:tada-idle} and~\ref{ex:paodr-envy} must be checked against the weaker reading, which we do there.

A circularity underlies everything: feasible reserve assignments depend on the open cutoff, which depends on who is left after reserve assignment. Score-feasibility is thus not a static eligibility condition and greedy quota-filling is unreliable.

\section{\uppercase{Student-Proposing Score-Elevated Mechanism}}
\label{sec:spse}

% \textcolor{red}{Section summary: DA/SPSE satisfies the usual DA properties, but it does not guarantee score-feasibility. This point should be visible before the propositions.}

Students apply to eligible categories and each keeps the highest adjusted-score applicants. SPSE inherits the usual DA advantages---feasibility, non-wastefulness, no justified envy, strategy-proofness---but may violate score-feasibility.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\footnotesize
\caption{Student-Proposing Score-Elevated Mechanism (SPSE)}
\label{alg:spse}
\KwIn{$I$, $V=T\cup\{o\}$, capacities $(q^c)_{c\in V}$, preferences $(\succ_i)_{i\in I}$, scores $\sigma$, eligibility $\T(\cdot)$, bonus $k$}
\KwOut{Matching $\mu=(\mu_c)_{c\in V}$}
$\mu_c\gets\emptyset$ for all $c\in V$; mark every student unmatched\;
Set $V_i:=\{o\}\cup\T(i)$ and compute $\sigma_c(i)$ for every $i$ and every $c\in V_i$\;
\While{some unmatched $i$ still has an unapplied category in $V_i$}{
    $i$ applies to her most preferred unapplied $c\in V_i$\;
    $c$ considers $\mu_c\cup\{i\}$ and keeps at most $q^c$ students with highest $\sigma_c$\;
    Every rejected student becomes unmatched\;
}
\Return{$\mu$}\;
\end{algorithm}

SPSE preserves one-to-one counting, a student being held by at most one category at a time, and differs from ordinary student-proposing deferred acceptance only in proposing to categories and in the reserve-side score elevation. Each student applies at most once per category, so the loop runs at most $|I|(|T|+1)$ times.

\begin{proposition}[Feasibility and non-wastefulness]
\label{prop:spse-basic}
The output of SPSE is feasible and non-wasteful in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:nw}.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Each category keeps at most its capacity, no student is held twice, and $i$ applies only within $V_i$: feasibility. For non-wastefulness, suppose unmatched $i$ could be added to $c\in V_i$ feasibly and score-feasibly; the enlargement needs $|\mu_c|<q^c$. Being unmatched at termination, $i$ applied to every category of $V_i$, including $c$, and was rejected. A category rejects only when full and thereafter only replaces a held student by a strictly better one, so $|\mu_c|=q^c$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[No justified envy]
\label{prop:spse-nje}
The output of SPSE has no justified envy, and is therefore fair across categories, fair within categories, and fair against unmatched claimants.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $\mu$ be the output and let $i$ and $c\in V_i$ satisfy clause (i) of Definition~\ref{def:nje}, so $c$ beats $i$'s final assignment. Applying in $\succ_i$-order and held only until rejected, she can end unmatched or worse off only after $c$ rejected her. At that rejection $c$ held $q^c$ students and dropped the lowest-$\sigma_c$ member of $\mu_c\cup\{i\}$, so every retained student beats $\sigma_c(i)$; thereafter $c$ never drops below $q^c$ and only improves. Both alternatives of clause (ii) fail, and the three consequences follow from Lemma~\ref{lem:hierarchy}.
\end{proof}

Raw scores and eligibility are verifiable, so the only object a student can misreport is $\succ_i$.

\begin{proposition}[Strategy-proofness]
\label{prop:spse-sp}
No student can obtain a strictly $\succ_i$-better assignment under SPSE by reporting an ordering over $V_i$ other than $\succ_i$, holding others' reports fixed.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Treat each $c\in V$ as a college of capacity $q^c$ whose priority over the students with $c\in V_i$ is the strict ranking induced by $\sigma_c$, well defined as raw scores are distinct and responsive since $c$ retains the $q^c$ highest available. Each $i$ ranks all of $V_i$ acceptable, so this is the college-admissions model of Gale and Shapley \cite{GaSh62a} with Algorithm~\ref{alg:spse} its student-proposing deferred acceptance; strategy-proofness for the proposing side is Dubins and Freedman \cite{DuFr81a} and Roth \cite{Roth82a}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[SPSE does not guarantee score-feasibility]
\label{prop:spse-not-sf}
The output of SPSE need not be score-feasible, even when a matching that is simultaneously score-feasible, non-wasteful and fair within categories exists.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $T=\{t_1,t_2\}$, $q^o=q^{t_1}=q^{t_2}=1$, $k=5$, $\delta=10$, and take $i_1(100,\emptyset)$, $i_2(95,\{t_1,t_2\})$, $i_3(82,\{t_2\})$ with $t_1\succ_{i_2}t_2\succ_{i_2}o$ and $t_2\succ_{i_3}o$, so $\sigma_{t_1}(i_2)=\sigma_{t_2}(i_2)=100$ and $\sigma_{t_2}(i_3)=87$. In round~1 each category receives one applicant and holds her, so SPSE terminates with $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$, $\mu_{t_1}=\{i_2\}$, $\mu_{t_2}=\{i_3\}$ and $e_o-e_{t_2}^+=13>\delta$.

Consider instead $\nu_o=\{i_1\}$, $\nu_{t_2}=\{i_2\}$, $\nu_{t_1}=\emptyset$, where $e_o(\nu)=e_{t_2}^+(\nu)=100$ and the empty $t_1$ is vacuous, so $\nu$ is score-feasible. It is non-wasteful, the only unmatched student $i_3$ having $V_{i_3}=\{t_2,o\}$ with both at capacity, and fair within categories, the sole member of $t_2$ satisfying $\sigma_{t_2}(i_2)=100>87$.
\end{proof}

We do not claim $\nu$ dominates: SPSE matches three students, $\nu$ two, and $\nu$ has justified envy, $i_2$ preferring the vacant $t_1$. The point is that the other properties do not force score-feasibility, which is an independent requirement bought here at the price of a seat---the trade-off Section~\ref{sec:tada} pays in coverage and Section~\ref{sec:paodr} in reserve capacity.

The instance also isolates the cause. A score-elevated policy can breach a cutoff-gap rule with no overlap at all, the sole eligible candidate simply being too weak; that is the inconsistency of \cite{AyTu23b}, and with $T=\{t_1\}$, $i_1(100,\emptyset)$ and $i_2(80,\{t_1\})$ preferring $t_1$, SPSE already returns a gap of $15>\delta$. Here nothing of the kind happens: $i_2$ has the same adjusted score in either category and alone would satisfy the rule in whichever she filled, so it is the routing of her single count, not the pool, that breaks it. Under single eligibility there is nothing to route, so the second cause is specific to overlap; Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} excludes the first by hypothesis.

\section{\uppercase{An Impossibility Driven by Overlap}}
\label{sec:impossibility}

An impossibility is easy to obtain for the uninteresting reason just described: in the two-student instance above, open-completeness forces $e_o=100$, nobody clears the bar, and reserve-completeness already fails. We therefore build the negation of that reason into the hypothesis.

\begin{definition}[Individual completability]
\label{def:indcomp}
An instance is \emph{individually completable} if for every $t\in T$ there is a feasible matching that is score-feasible, satisfies open-completeness, and has $|\mu_t|=q^t$, where open-completeness requires $\mu_o$ to consist of the $q^o$ highest raw-score students in $I$.
\end{definition}

So every announced quota is separately honourable in full, score-feasibly and without disturbing the open category. The impossibility is that the promises cannot be kept together.

\begin{proposition}[Impossibility under full capacity utilization]
\label{prop:impossibility}
There is an individually completable instance (Definition~\ref{def:indcomp}) admitting no feasible matching that simultaneously satisfies score-feasibility, \emph{open-completeness} and \emph{reserve-completeness}, where reserve-completeness requires $|\mu_t|=q^t$ for every $t$ for which at least $q^t$ students are eligible.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $T=\{t_1,t_2\}$, $q^o=q^{t_1}=q^{t_2}=1$, $k=5$, $\delta=10$, and take $i_1(100,\emptyset)$, $i_2(95,\{t_1,t_2\})$, $i_3(80,\{t_1\})$, $i_4(78,\{t_2\})$. Open-completeness forces $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$, so $e_o(\mu)=100$ and score-feasibility requires $\sigma_t(i)\ge90$ for every reserve assignee. Since $\sigma_{t_1}(i_2)=\sigma_{t_2}(i_2)=100\ge90$ while $\sigma_{t_1}(i_3)=85<90$ and $\sigma_{t_2}(i_4)=83<90$, student $i_2$ is the only one placeable in any reserve category.

It is individually completable: $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$, $\mu_{t_1}=\{i_2\}$, $\mu_{t_2}=\emptyset$ is open-complete with $|\mu_{t_1}|=q^{t_1}$ and a gap of $0$, the empty $t_2$ being vacuous, and exchanging $t_1$ and $t_2$ handles $t_2$. Yet both quotas cannot be met at once: the eligible sets are $\{i_2,i_3\}$ and $\{i_2,i_4\}$, so reserve-completeness demands two distinct reserve placements while one-to-one counting lets $i_2$ count toward one. Some reserve category must then admit $i_3$ or $i_4$, breaching score-feasibility.
\end{proof}

Both axioms are institutional commitments rather than normative optima: the open category is genuinely open, and an announced quota is honoured whenever the eligible population suffices---which is how policy documents write quotas, counting category by category precisely because they are drafted without reference to overlap. Weakening reserve-completeness to what is \emph{jointly} fillable would restore consistency but concede the point, that overlap silently reduces what a stated quota delivers; PA-ODR makes that concession explicit as de-reservation. The obstruction is worth naming: in the feasibility graph of Section~\ref{sec:paodr} at $e=100$ the two categories share the neighbourhood $\{i_2\}$, so the capacitated Hall condition $|N(S)|\ge\sum_{t\in S}q^t$ fails at $S=T$ while holding on singletons, which is individual completability. That gap is exactly overlap, and it is why PA-ODR is built on a bipartite matching: an obstruction on sets of categories is invisible to a rule processing them one at a time.

\section{\uppercase{Adaptive Score-Threshold Deferred Acceptance}}
\label{sec:tada}

Read in realized cutoffs, Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} leaves three ways out: abandon open-completeness, reserve-completeness, or the announced capacities. This section takes the middle option and the next the last. A-STDA keeps $q$ untouched and declines to fill a reserve seat it cannot fill legally, so the seat stays empty and on the books.

For a threshold $\tau\in\R\cup\{-\infty\}$, let the \emph{admissible application set} of $i$ be
\[
A_i(\tau):=\{o\}\cup\{t\in\T(i):\sigma(i)+k\ge\tau-\delta\},
\]
which depends on scores and eligibility but not on preferences; under a common bonus it contains all of $\T(i)$ or none of it. Student-proposing deferred acceptance with $A_i(\tau)$ in place of $V_i$ and capacities $q$ yields a matching $\mathrm{DA}(\tau)$. \emph{Adaptive score-threshold deferred acceptance} (A-STDA), Algorithm~\ref{alg:tada}, adapts $\tau$ to the open cutoff its own outcome induces and stops at a fixed point, so each round admits exactly those students the previous round's open cutoff permits. Adapting an eligibility rule to the cutoff it produces is the device behind dynamic reserves \cite{AyTu20a}; here it is applied to the score-admissible sets, every round being student-proposing deferred acceptance \cite{GaSh62a,Roth08a}.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\footnotesize
\caption{Adaptive Score-Threshold Deferred Acceptance (A-STDA)}
\label{alg:tada}
\KwIn{$I$, $V=T\cup\{o\}$, capacities $(q^c)_{c\in V}$, preferences $(\succ_i)_{i\in I}$, scores $\sigma$, eligibility $\T(\cdot)$, bonus $k$, gap $\delta$}
\KwOut{Matching $\mu=(\mu_c)_{c\in V}$}
$\tau\gets-\infty$\;
\While{true}{
    $A_i(\tau)\gets\{o\}\cup\{t\in\T(i):\sigma(i)+k\ge\tau-\delta\}$ for every $i$\;
    $\mu\gets$ student-proposing DA on $(A_i(\tau))_{i\in I}$ with capacities $q$\;
    \lIf{$e_o(\mu)\le\tau$}{\Return{$\mu$}}
    $\tau\gets e_o(\mu)$\;
}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{proposition}[Properties of A-STDA]
\label{prop:tada-props}
A-STDA terminates after at most $|I|+1$ deferred-acceptance runs. Its output is feasible with respect to the original capacities $q$, score-feasible in the realized cutoffs of Definition~\ref{def:scorefeas}, and fair across categories in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:across}, hence also fair within categories. Moreover no unmatched student can be added to the open category.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Termination.} The threshold changes only by rising to $e_o(\mu)$, a realized open cutoff and hence a raw score, so it never repeats a value and after at most $|I|$ updates the test $e_o(\mu)\le\tau$ must succeed. Write $\tau^\ast$ for the terminal threshold, so $\mu=\mathrm{DA}(\tau^\ast)$ and $e_o(\mu)\le\tau^\ast$.

\emph{Feasibility} is immediate: deferred acceptance never holds more than $q^c$ students at $c$ nor a student at two categories, and proposals run inside $A_i(\tau^\ast)\subseteq V_i$. \emph{Score-feasibility}: every $i\in\mu_t$ proposed to $t$, so $\sigma_t(i)\ge\tau^\ast-\delta\ge e_o(\mu)-\delta$, and empty categories are vacuous by Definition~\ref{def:scorefeas}.

\emph{Across-category fairness.} Suppose $c\in V_i$, $j\in\mu_c$, $c\succ_i\mu(i)$ and $\sigma_c(i)>\sigma_c(j)$. If $c\in A_i(\tau^\ast)$, then $i$ proposes in $\succ_i$-order inside $A_i(\tau^\ast)$ and ends worse off than $c$, so she proposed to $c$ and was rejected; a category rejects only when full and afterwards only improves, so every $j'\in\mu_c$ has $\sigma_c(j')>\sigma_c(i)$, contradicting the choice of $j$. If instead $c\notin A_i(\tau^\ast)$, then $c$ is a reserve category $t$ with $\sigma(i)+k<\tau^\ast-\delta$, since $o\in A_i(\tau^\ast)$ always; but $j$ proposed to $t$, so $\sigma(j)+k\ge\tau^\ast-\delta>\sigma(i)+k$, again a contradiction. Within-category fairness follows by Lemma~\ref{lem:hierarchy}.

\emph{The open category.} $o\in A_i(\tau^\ast)$ for every $i$, so an unmatched $i$ proposed to $o$ and was rejected, giving $|\mu_o|=q^o$.
\end{proof}

No fixed-point property of $\tau^\ast$ beyond the terminal test is needed: the case analysis runs at $\tau^\ast$ itself. The second case is why across-category fairness survives the restriction of proposals: a student the threshold silences is one every reserve admit already outscores. What A-STDA does not inherit is the rest of the deferred-acceptance package, because it fills no seat it cannot fill legally.

\begin{example}[A-STDA idles a seat, and the idle seat is both a waste and an envy]
\label{ex:tada-idle}
Take $T=\{t_1\}$, $q^o=q^{t_1}=1$, $k=5$, $\delta=10$, $i_1(100,\emptyset)$ and $i_2(80,\{t_1\})$ with $t_1\succ_{i_2}o$. At $\tau=-\infty$ every category is admissible and DA gives $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$, $\mu_{t_1}=\{i_2\}$, whose gap is $100-85=15>\delta$; as $e_o(\mu)=100>\tau$ the threshold rises to $100$. At $\tau=100$ we have $85<90$, so $A_{i_2}(100)=\{o\}$ and DA gives $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$, $\mu_{t_1}=\emptyset$ with $e_o(\mu)=100\le\tau$. A-STDA stops there, leaving $t_1$ empty although $i_2$ is eligible and unmatched; the outcome is unchanged if $i_2$ ranks $o$ first.

The empty seat is a plain waste, $|\mu_{t_1}|=0<q^{t_1}$ with $t_1\in V_{i_2}$, but not a waste under Definition~\ref{def:nw}, since adding $i_2$ restores the gap of $15$. The example separates the two readings and shows the score-relative clause is no bookkeeping convention: it is the difference between calling this outcome wasteful and calling it constrained. The seat also witnesses justified envy, $i_2\notin M(\mu)$ with $|\mu_{t_1}|<q^{t_1}$ meeting both clauses of Definition~\ref{def:nje}. What she points at, though, is a \emph{vacant} seat and not a weaker occupant: A-STDA loses Definition~\ref{def:nje} exactly on the vacancy clause that Definition~\ref{def:across} drops, and stays fair across categories by Proposition~\ref{prop:tada-props}---the first strictness claim of Lemma~\ref{lem:hierarchy}.

It separates the two cutoff readings too. Here $t_1$ is undersubscribed, so $\tilde e_{t_1}^+(\mu)=0$ while $\tilde e_o(\mu)=100>\delta$, and the outcome is not effectively score-feasible: a school leaving a quota unfilled has set no bar there and has thereby breached the gap rule it was protecting. A-STDA is score-feasible in realized cutoffs only, the price of keeping $q$ fixed. Under the effective reading Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} loses this escape: giving up reserve-completeness no longer restores the cutoff rule, and de-reservation---costing no seat---is the only remedy that keeps the open category open.
\end{example}

Non-wastefulness is settled only at the open category. At a reserve category $t\notin A_i(\tau^\ast)$ the student was barred from proposing, and adding her gives $e_t^+\le\sigma(i)+k<\tau^\ast-\delta$, so the enlarged matching is score-feasible only if $e_o(\mu)<\tau^\ast$. Definition~\ref{def:nw} therefore holds outright whenever $\tau^\ast\le e_o(\mu)$; whether the converse can occur we have not settled, and it did not in any instance of Section~\ref{sec:experiments}. PA-ODR's cutoff is the realized one by construction, so the question does not arise there.

% [AI] Glit26a の incentive 分析が示唆を与えないか要確認（TODO.md 参照）。
Whether A-STDA is strategy-proof we leave open, and the question is delicate rather than neglected. At a \emph{fixed} threshold the mechanism is deferred acceptance on the preference-independent sets $A_i(\tau)$, hence strategy-proof by Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-sp}; but the terminal threshold is the open cutoff of a deferred-acceptance outcome and so is itself preference-dependent. We searched $210{,}000$ randomized instances, $150{,}000$ of them drawn to favour exactly this configuration, and found no profitable misreport; the same search finds one for PA-ODR within a few thousand. We record that the naive argument for strategy-proofness fails.

\section{\uppercase{Preference-Aware Overlapping De-Reservation}}
\label{sec:paodr}

% \textcolor{red}{Add a brief summary of what PA-ODR does and which properties it satisfies/fails.}

PA-ODR fixes the open cutoff, assigns reserves only along score-feasible pairs, and de-reserves unfulfillable reserve capacity to the open category. It is score-feasible but changes capacities and loses the DA-style incentive and envy guarantees. Modifying announced capacities to restore existence is the near-feasibility paradigm, and the incentive loss of Section~\ref{sec:paodr-limits} is one it is known to invite \cite{Glit26a}.

\subsection{Feasibility Graph and Lexicographic Matching}

For a fixed open cutoff $e$, let $G(e)$ be the bipartite graph with left side the students not in $\mu_o$, right side $T$, and an edge $(i,t)$ iff $t\in\T(i)$ and $\sigma(i)+k\ge e-\delta$; under a common bonus a student is score-admissible at $e$ for all her categories or none. Write $P(e)$ for the edge set and fix an injective index $p:P(e)\to\{1,\dots,|P(e)|\}$ from linear orders on $I$ and $T$. Among capacitated matchings on $G(e)$, \textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching} is the one selected by, in order: (1)~maximize the number of matched pairs; (2)~lexicographically maximize the weakly decreasingly sorted vector of matched elevated scores $\sigma_t(i)=\sigma(i)+k$; (3)~lexicographically minimize the weakly decreasingly sorted vector of preference ranks $r_i(t)$; (4)~minimize $\sum_{(i,t)\in\mu}2^{p(i,t)}$.

Criterion 3 compares matchings by worst preference rank first, so it is egalitarian. Criterion 4 is a pure tie-breaker and is needed: two students eligible for the same two unit-capacity categories and ranking them alike give assignments with identical cardinality, score and rank vectors. Distinct edge sets give distinct sums of distinct powers of two, so it always resolves such ties. Criteria 1--2 already pin down \emph{who} is admitted, leaving 3--4 to decide only \emph{where}.

\begin{lemma}[Criteria 1 and 2 determine the admitted set]
\label{lem:matroid}
Fix $e$ and right-side capacities $(\hat q^t)_{t\in T}$. All capacitated matchings on $G(e)$ satisfying criteria 1 and 2 match exactly the same set of students; criteria 3 and 4 only choose its assignment to categories.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Replace each $t$ by $\hat q^t$ parallel copies, turning capacitated matchings into ordinary ones. The matchable student sets are exactly the independent sets of the induced transversal matroid, and criterion 1 restricts to its bases. Weight $i$ by $\sigma(i)+k$, pairwise distinct as raw scores are. Criterion 2 selects the bases whose decreasingly sorted weight vector is lexicographically maximal, which for distinct weights is the output of the greedy algorithm scanning students in decreasing weight; that algorithm is deterministic, so the basis is unique.
\end{proof}

So preferences matter only after size and score are optimized. The matroid structure is that underlying \cite{Flei03a} and the substitutable choice functions of \cite{GKK+17a,KoSo16a}; specific here is a ground set defined by an endogenous cutoff. \textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching} is computable in polynomial time: weighting each admissible pair so the four criteria occupy decreasing orders of magnitude---possible with $O(|I||T|)$-bit weights, the common bonus making $\sigma_t(i)$ independent of $t$---reduces it to one maximum-weight capacitated bipartite matching. By Lemma~\ref{lem:matroid} an implementation may instead take the admitted set by greedy and solve a smaller min-cost assignment, as our prototype does.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\footnotesize
\caption{Preference-Aware Overlapping De-Reservation (PA-ODR)}
\label{alg:paodr}
\KwIn{$I$, scores $\sigma$, eligibility $\T(\cdot)$, preferences $(\succ_i)_{i\in I}$, capacities $q^o,(q^t)_{t\in T}$, bonus $k$, gap $\delta$, a fixed linear order $\prec$ on $T$}
\KwOut{Matching $\mu=(\mu_o,(\mu_t)_{t\in T})$}
$\hat q^o\gets q^o$ and $\hat q^t\gets q^t$ for all $t\in T$\;
\While{true}{
    Assign the top $\min\{\hat q^o,|I|\}$ raw-score students to $o$; call this set $\mu_o$\;
    $e\gets\min_{i\in\mu_o}\sigma(i)$ if $\mu_o\neq\emptyset$, else $e\gets-\infty$\;
    Build $G(e)$ with edge $(i,t)$ iff $t\in\T(i)$ and $\sigma(i)+k\ge e-\delta$, giving $t$ capacity $\hat q^t$\;
    Compute $(\mu_t)_{t\in T}$ by \textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching}\;
    \eIf{there exists $t$ with $|\mu_t|<\hat q^t$}{
        Let $t^\ast$ be the $\prec$-first such category\;
        $\hat q^{t^\ast}\gets\hat q^{t^\ast}-1$ and $\hat q^o\gets\hat q^o+1$\;
    }{
        \Return{$\mu=(\mu_o,(\mu_t)_{t\in T})$}\;
    }
}
\end{algorithm}

Algorithm~\ref{alg:paodr} builds PA-ODR on \textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching}. It treats score-feasibility as a hard institutional constraint and is a fallback mechanism, not a quota-preserving reserve rule: the open category is filled first by raw score, reserve assignment runs only over pairs feasible at the induced cutoff, and capacity a reserve category cannot fill is de-reserved to the open category. Merit ordering in the open category is preserved and preferences only break ties among feasible reserve placements; the order $\prec$ is part of the specification and makes the output well defined. PA-ODR is preference-aware only on the reserve side, so a student preferring a reserve category to $o$ may still be placed in $o$, which costs it both envy-freeness and strategy-proofness (Section~\ref{sec:paodr-limits}).

Each non-terminating iteration raises $\hat q^o$ by one, so the open sets are nested and the cutoffs $e$ fall weakly. Two opposing forces follow: a lower $e$ only adds edges to $G(e)$, but the same step moves one more student into $\mu_o$ and only removes left vertices. Neither dominates, so a single de-reservation can fail to help---the step that frees one category can absorb the only student eligible for another. How many categories PA-ODR retires therefore depends on $\prec$, and it does not minimize de-reservation.

\subsection{Properties of PA-ODR}

With de-reservation in the model (Definition~\ref{def:dereserve}), feasibility and non-wastefulness must be judged against the final vector $\hat q$ rather than the initial $q$; Section~\ref{sec:experiments} reports how many quotas this retires, so the qualifier is a genuine weakening and not a bookkeeping convention.

\begin{definition}[Non-wastefulness relative to the final effective capacities]
\label{def:nw-hat}
Let $\hat q$ be the final effective capacity vector produced by PA-ODR. A matching $\mu$ is \emph{non-wasteful relative to $\hat q$} if there are no unmatched $i$ and $c\in V_i$ such that adding $i$ to $c$ yields a matching feasible with respect to $\hat q$ and score-feasible.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Termination, running time, feasibility, score-feasibility, non-wastefulness]
\label{prop:paodr-basic}
PA-ODR terminates; after replacing each $q^c$ by $\min\{q^c,|I|\}$ it runs in time polynomial in the input size. Its output is feasible with respect to the final effective capacities, score-feasible in the effective cutoffs of Definition~\ref{def:effcutoff} and hence in the realized ones, and non-wasteful relative to them (Definition~\ref{def:nw-hat}).
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Termination.} A non-terminating iteration moves one seat from a reserve category to $o$, so the nonnegative integer $\sum_t\hat q^t$ falls by one each time and the loop stops after at most $\sum_t q^t$ iterations.

\emph{Running time.} That bound is exponential if capacities are binary-encoded, so we assume throughout that $q^c\le|I|$ for every $c\in V$; this is an assumption rather than a normalization, since it changes how many de-reservation steps the loop takes. Then $\sum_t\hat q^t\le|I||T|$, giving $O(|I||T|)$ iterations, each one sort, one $G(e)$ and one \textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching}.

\emph{Feasibility.} In every iteration $o$ receives the top $\min\{\hat q^o,|I|\}$ students, reserve assignment runs on the rest with capacities $\hat q^t$, and edges of $G(e)$ join $i$ only to $t\in\T(i)$, so no student is assigned twice and no category is over capacity.

\emph{Score-feasibility.} Let $e$ be the final cutoff, so $e_o(\mu)=e$. Every reserve assignee sits on an edge of $G(e)$, hence $\sigma(i)+k\ge e-\delta$ and $e_t^+(\mu)\ge e_o(\mu)-\delta$ for nonempty $t$; empty categories are vacuous. The effective version follows because the loop exits only with $|\mu_t|=\hat q^t$ for every $t$, so $\tilde e_t^+(\mu)$ is $e_t^+(\mu)$ or $+\infty$, while $|\mu_o|<\hat q^o$ forces $\mu_o=I$ and $\tilde e_o(\mu)=0$.

\emph{Non-wastefulness.} $\mu_o$ holds the top $\min\{\hat q^o,|I|\}$ students, so either everyone is matched or nobody can be added to $o$; and $|\mu_t|=\hat q^t$ at termination, since otherwise another de-reservation would have occurred. No unmatched student can be added anywhere within $\hat q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Fairness against unmatched claimants]
\label{prop:paodr-fair}
The output of PA-ODR is fair against unmatched claimants in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:fair-unm}.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Fix the final iteration, with cutoff $e$ and output $\mu$, and suppose $t\in T$ and $i,j$ satisfy $t\in\T(i)\cap\T(j)$, $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$, $j\in\mu_t$ and $i\notin M(\mu)$. First, $(i,t)$ is an edge of $G(e)$: being unmatched, $i$ is on the left side; $(j,t)$ is an edge, so $\sigma(j)+k\ge e-\delta$, and $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$ gives $\sigma(i)+k\ge e-\delta$. This is the crucial step: a student outranking a matched student inside a category is automatically admissible at the prevailing cutoff, so the cutoff never shields a matched student from a stronger claimant. Replacing $j$ by $i$ in $\mu_t$ now gives a capacitated matching on $G(e)$ of the same cardinality whose sorted score vector is lexicographically strictly larger, contradicting criteria 1 and 2.
\end{proof}

The qualifier ``with respect to the final capacity vector'' attaches to Proposition~\ref{prop:paodr-basic} but not to this one: PA-ODR satisfies Definition~\ref{def:fair-unm} unweakened, as SPSE does. The reason is Lemma~\ref{lem:fair-sf} with the first step above---an unmatched claimant's upward claim can neither break score-feasibility nor be blocked by the cutoff. Unlike SPSE, though, it comes from optimality inside the final feasibility graph rather than from rejection monotonicity, and by Proposition~\ref{prop:paodr-unfair} it is as far up Lemma~\ref{lem:hierarchy} as PA-ODR reaches.

\subsection{What PA-ODR Gives Up}
\label{sec:paodr-limits}

Enforcing score-feasibility is not free; the price falls on the fairness and incentive side. The source is common to both: PA-ODR settles the reserve assignment by cardinality and score first and consults preferences only as a tie-breaker, and it fills the open category by raw score without consulting them at all.

\begin{example}[PA-ODR admits justified envy]
\label{ex:paodr-envy}
Let $T=\{t_1\}$, $q^o=q^{t_1}=1$, $k=5$, $\delta=10$, with $i_1(100,\{t_1\})$ and $i_2(90,\{t_1\})$ both ranking $t_1$ above $o$. PA-ODR gives $o$ the top raw score, so $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$ and $e=100$; as $95\ge90$, $(i_2,t_1)$ is an edge, $\mu_{t_1}=\{i_2\}$, every capacity is met and the algorithm stops without de-reserving, score-feasibly ($e_o-e_{t_1}^+=5\le\delta$). Yet $i_1$ sits in $o$ while strictly preferring $t_1$, whose occupant satisfies $\sigma_{t_1}(i_2)=95<105=\sigma_{t_1}(i_1)$. The triple $(i_1,t_1,i_2)$ therefore blocks within categories, across categories and by justified envy at once, while Definition~\ref{def:fair-unm} holds, every student being matched: the third strictness claim of Lemma~\ref{lem:hierarchy}.

The score rule does not excuse the failure, which matters because the three notions were left unweakened. Honouring the claim gives $\mu_{t_1}=\{i_1\}$, $\mu_o=\{i_2\}$ with $e_o=90$ and $e_{t_1}^+=105$, so the alternative is score-feasible and the complaint survives the score-relative reading. It is also exactly what SPSE returns here, both students applying to $t_1$, which retains $i_1$ and rejects $i_2$. The mechanisms differ purely in whose claim they honour, with no feasibility excuse for either.
\end{example}

\begin{proposition}[PA-ODR reaches no further up the hierarchy]
\label{prop:paodr-unfair}
The output of PA-ODR need not be fair within categories in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:fair}, hence need not be fair across categories either. This already happens with $|T|=1$, and it happens with the complaint confined to the reserve categories, in an instance where every quota is filled and nothing is de-reserved.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The first claim is Example~\ref{ex:paodr-envy}. For the second let $T=\{t_1,t_2\}$, $q^o=q^{t_1}=q^{t_2}=1$, $k=5$, $\delta=10$, with $i_1(100,\emptyset)$, $i_2(95,\{t_1,t_2\})$, $i_3(90,\{t_2\})$ and $t_2\succ_{i_2}t_1\succ_{i_2}o$. Then $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$, $e=100$, admissibility needs $\sigma(i)\ge85$, and $G(100)$ carries $(i_2,t_1)$, $(i_2,t_2)$, $(i_3,t_2)$. Only $\{(i_2,t_1),(i_3,t_2)\}$ has cardinality $2$, so criterion 1 alone fixes the outcome, every quota is met and the loop exits at $\hat q=q$. But $\sigma_{t_2}(i_2)=100>95=\sigma_{t_2}(i_3)$ with $i_3\in\mu_{t_2}$ and $t_2\succ_{i_2}t_1=\mu(i_2)$: a within-blocking triple in which the open category plays no part. Honouring it by $\mu_{t_2}=\{i_2\}$, $\mu_{t_1}=\emptyset$ gives $e_o=100=e_{t_2}^+$, so this complaint too survives the score-relative reading.
\end{proof}

The failure is structural, not a defect of the tie-breaker: criterion 1 maximizes reserve placements, and a strong overlapping student is the cheapest cover for a category only she can reach, so she is spent where she is scarce rather than where she is wanted. What criterion 3 does secure, between reserve categories, is the weaker exchange requirement that no two matched students each prefer the other's category. Were $i\in\mu_c$ and $j\in\mu_{c'}$ with $c,c'\in T$ such a pair, admissibility at $e$ being category-independent makes $(i,c')$ and $(j,c)$ edges too, so exchanging them keeps the matched set and leaves criteria 1 and 2 indifferent while the sorted rank vector falls in two coordinates and rises in none, contradicting criterion 3. That requirement cannot express the complaint above, where $i_3$ has no claim on $t_1$ to trade away.

\begin{proposition}[PA-ODR is not strategy-proof]
\label{prop:paodr-notsp}
There is an instance in which a student obtains a strictly $\succ_i$-better assignment under PA-ODR by misreporting her category preference.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $T=\{t_1,t_2\}$, $q^o=q^{t_1}=q^{t_2}=1$, $k=5$, $\delta=10$, with $i_1(100,\emptyset)$, $i_2(95,\{t_1,t_2\})$, $i_3(94,\{t_1,t_2\})$ and true preferences $t_1\succ_{i_2}t_2\succ_{i_2}o$, $o\succ_{i_3}t_1\succ_{i_3}t_2$. In every run $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$, $e=100$, and both $i_2$ and $i_3$ clear the bar, so $G(100)$ has all four edges between $\{i_2,i_3\}$ and $\{t_1,t_2\}$ and by Lemma~\ref{lem:matroid} both are matched. Criterion 2 is silent and criterion 3 decides between $\alpha:i_2\mapsto t_1,\ i_3\mapsto t_2$ and $\beta:i_2\mapsto t_2,\ i_3\mapsto t_1$. Truthfully the sorted rank vectors are $(3,1)$ for $\alpha$ and $(2,2)$ for $\beta$, so criterion 3 picks $\beta$ and $i_2$ gets $t_2$. Let $i_2$ instead report $t_1\succ o\succ t_2$, demoting $t_2$ below $o$ while keeping $t_1$ on top: the vectors become $(3,1)$ and $(3,2)$, which agree in the first coordinate, so criterion 3 picks $\alpha$ and $i_2$ gets $t_1$. As truly $t_1\succ_{i_2}t_2$, the misreport makes her strictly better off.
\end{proof}

This is the manipulation any egalitarian tie-breaker invites: reporting a category as worse than it is makes every assignment giving it to her look worse, which under a min--max rule pays whenever she would otherwise be sacrificed. It is not an artifact of criterion 3 but reflects that PA-ODR is no deferred-acceptance mechanism---and by Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} cannot be replaced by one holding capacities fixed.

