This is not evidence of average-case performance; it prices the trade-off Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} forces.

First, SPSE breaches the cutoff rule in $46$ and $55$ of the two families' instances, so Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf} is neither an artifact of three-student examples nor something scale washes out.

Second, the score-relative reading of non-wastefulness earns its keep. A-STDA satisfies Definition~\ref{def:nw} in all $200$ instances while violating the plain version in $46$ and $54$: the idle seats of Example~\ref{ex:tada-idle} are common, not pathological. Neither SPSE nor PA-ODR ever separates the two readings, so without A-STDA the weakening would have no witness. The rates line up with SPSE's breach rates, which is no coincidence: A-STDA idles a seat roughly when SPSE would have used it illegally.

Third, across-category fairness is where the mechanisms part, and it separates cleanly from no justified envy. A-STDA is fair across categories in all $200$ of its runs yet admits justified envy in $46$ and $55$: every one of those complaints points at a seat it deliberately left vacant, the clause Definition~\ref{def:across} drops. PA-ODR is the mirror image, fair across categories in $0$ and $63$, its two columns coinciding exactly, as it stops only with every surviving quota filled and so never leaves a vacancy to complain about; its failures are the substantive kind, against a weaker occupant. The across column agreed with within-category fairness in all $600$ runs, so PA-ODR's losses are not confined to the open category.

Fourth, and this is what the capacity-poor family conceals, the two responses to Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} charge in different currencies. Where capacity binds, PA-ODR matches as many students as SPSE and pays purely in quota, $39\%$ of the reserve seats, while A-STDA pays purely in coverage, $4.79$ students, and keeps every seat. Where capacity is slack the gap widens: PA-ODR matches $56.61$ students, more than SPSE's $55.48$ and exactly the $\min\{\sum_c q^c,|I|\}$ bound it saturates by construction---but it costs $93\%$ of the reserve seats to buy $1.13$ students, while A-STDA retires nothing and matches $46.83$. Whether that is a good bargain is a policy question; what the model contributes is that the bargain must be struck.

Fifth, the price tracks $\delta$: on the capacity-poor family, $\delta=2,4,8,15,25$ leave SPSE score-feasible in $34,43,54,79,97$ instances and retire $30.32,26.38,18.57,7.84,0.61$ of the $47.99$ reserve seats, so at a permissive gap all three nearly coincide.

Two caveats. Running times are medians on one laptop, reported only to show that nothing here is intractable; A-STDA reached its fixed point in two rounds in every instance. And the breach rates mix the two causes of Section~\ref{sec:spse}: lowering the overlap probability to $0.15$ leaves SPSE score-feasible in only $6$ of $100$ capacity-poor instances, worse rather than better, thin eligibility stranding weak candidates. The breach rate is therefore not a measure of the overlap-specific cause of Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf}.

\section{\uppercase{Conclusion}}

Overlapping reserve categories create a genuine allocation problem because admitted students count toward exactly one category. Score constraints make feasibility depend on endogenous cutoffs, so non-wastefulness must be read relative to the constraint, and full capacity utilization can fail even when every quota is individually honourable. Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} forces a choice: score-feasibility, open-completeness and reserve-completeness cannot be had together. SPSE gives up score-feasibility, keeping the deferred-acceptance package; A-STDA gives up reserve-completeness, leaving announced seats empty; PA-ODR gives up the announced capacities; Tables~\ref{tab:comparison} and~\ref{tab:prototype} record what each keeps and what each costs. Across-category fairness divides them differently from no justified envy: SPSE and A-STDA keep it, the latter \emph{without} keeping no justified envy, the only complaint it admits being against a seat it left vacant. PA-ODR keeps neither, and not merely at the open category: maximizing reserve placements spends a strong overlapping student where she is scarce rather than where she is wanted (Proposition~\ref{prop:paodr-unfair}).

We should not overstate what overlap explains: cutoff-gap breaches and order dependence occur without it. The specifically overlapping content is the routing of multiply eligible students and the Hall obstruction of Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility}: the gap between Hall's condition on singleton category sets and on larger ones.

